<speak>
<p>Recall the earlier factorization: n equals two to the two s times w squared, with s at least one and w odd. The original parameter u equals two to the s minus one times w. Assume, for contradiction, that s is at least two. Under that assumption, the preceding descent would leave a cycle of length M equal to two to the s plus one, with odd coefficients b sub j and scalar norm four w squared.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Read the identity coefficient of the final polynomial times its star. A term b sub j times X to the j meets a starred term b sub ell times X to the negative ell. Their product returns to the identity position exactly when j minus ell is zero modulo M. With both indices between zero and M minus one, this means j equals ell. Matching pairs therefore contribute the squares b sub j squared. Collecting them gives a sum of M odd squares equal to four w squared. Our eight position illustration shows the smallest possible endpoint.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Now reduce modulo eight. Write an odd integer b as two r plus one. Its square is one plus four times the product of the consecutive integers r and r plus one. One of those integers is even, so their product is even. The multiple of four is therefore a multiple of eight. Every odd square has residue one.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Replace each square on the left by one. Since s is at least two, M equals eight times two to the s minus two. Group the ones into blocks of eight. Every block has residue zero, so the whole left side has residue zero.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>But w is odd as well. Its square has residue one, so four w squared has residue four modulo eight. The same integer equation would require zero to equal four. That is impossible. The conditional descent endpoint cannot exist.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Therefore s cannot be at least two. We already had s at least one, so s equals one. Substitute this into the original u: two to the zero times w equals w, which is odd. The order becomes n equals four u squared, with u odd. This completes the order restriction.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>The case u equals one gives order four and survives this argument. Its known circulant Hadamard matrix remains an example. The remaining proof will assume u greater than one and exclude those larger candidates.<break time="900ms"/></p>
</speak>
